course: dictionary
lesson: 3368
title: Exclusively: only, and nobody else
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word «exclusively» means only, for one person, group or thing and nobody or nothing else. «The shop sells this game exclusively online.» The clue is «online»: you can buy the game there, and not anywhere else.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The animal eats exclusively plants» tells you it never eats meat, because «exclusively» means there are no exceptions. Try the swap test: put «only» in place of «exclusively». If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: Kipling keeps saying «'sclusively», his baby-talk for «exclusively», which here means «only, completely». Even a shortened, funny version still carries the same meaning. When a word sounds strange, look for the clue in the rest of the sentence.
fact: Exclusively is an adverb, made from the adjective «exclusive».
fact: «Exclusively» means only, for one person, group or thing and nobody or nothing else.
fact: «Exclusively» can also mean completely and only, as in «made exclusively of wood».
fact: It tells the reader that there are no exceptions.
fact: Common phrases are «exclusively for», «almost exclusively» and «exclusively available».
exercise: «The shop sells this game exclusively online.» What does exclusively mean here? | *Only, not in any other place | Often, many times | Slowly and carefully | Very quickly
exercise: «The chair is made exclusively of wood.» What does exclusively tell us? | *Completely and only wood | Partly wood and partly metal | Painted in bright colours | Nearly wood, with a few bits of something else
exercise: Kipling says «'sclusively». What word does he mean? | Explosively | *Exclusively | Exactly | Excitedly
test: «The museum is open to members exclusively on Monday.» What does exclusively mean here? | Open to the public at lunchtime | Free for everyone | Closed all day | *Only for members
test: «The offer is exclusively available to students.» What does this tell the reader? | *There are no exceptions: only students | Students get it for free only sometimes | Everyone can have it | It is a rare kind of offer
exam: Which word in «The animal eats exclusively plants» tells the reader there are no exceptions? | plants | eats | animal | *exclusively
